 ## Background
-Bricking was developed to address the limitations of early GPU hardware, which had small texture memory capacities (e.g., 128 MB on VolumePro PCI cards) and limits on texture dimensions (e.g., 256 voxels per edge) [S3, unofficial]. The VolumePro system (1999) popularized the "supervolume" concept, where software partitions the volume, handles data duplication, and composites results [S3, unofficial]. Later, with the rise of general-purpose GPU computing, bricking was adapted for GPU-based raycasting to handle massive out-of-core datasets [S4]. It is used in parallel rendering software like ParaView, where it serves as a form of data parallelism [S1, unofficial].
